blocks.en:
MRS is an under-specified computational semantic representation. Rather than encoding every scope decision directly in a single recursively nested formula, MRS represents elementary predications, their arguments, handles, and constraints among possible scopes (see Appendix 0.A for a full example). This permits a parser to construct a compact representation while deferring some scope decisions to a subsequent resolution stage. These resolutions can be represented as scope
trees , which specify the relative scope of quantifiers and other
scope-bearing operators. For the scope ambiguity in Example 1 , two simplified scope trees deciding the order of quantifiers (ignoring the telescope clause) would be: {forest} {forest}

